(ns flint.check
  "Checks that cost nothing in a release build.

  Two things at once, and they are the same thing seen from two sides:

  * **Inline checks** against bad usage, so a library says what went wrong
    where it went wrong rather than failing three frames later with a message
    about a type nobody passed.
  * **Unit checks**, collected by `flint test`, written in the namespace they
    are about rather than in a parallel tree.

  ## Always resolvable, two variants

  This namespace ALWAYS EXISTS (`DECISIONS.md#checks`): it is a compiler root
  like `flint.port`/`flint.wire`, answered by the front door from its own
  built-in copy before any host resolver is ever asked, whatever `features`
  a compile runs with. What changes between builds is not whether
  `flint.check` is present, but which of two variants each of its public
  names resolves to, chosen by the single `#?(:flint/check A :default B)` on
  each one below -- the same deferred-`#?` mechanism every other stdlib file
  already uses to serve one pre-read copy to every feature set:

  * **the ON variant** (`:flint/check` in `features`, the default): the real
    implementation described below.
  * **the OFF variant** (`:optimize [perf]`, or `:checks false`): every
    public name still exists, with the SAME arity, so code that mentions
    `flint.check/expect` or `flint.check/run-tests` with no
    `#?(:flint/check ...)` wrapper and no `:require` compiles in both
    builds. Each one is either a MACRO that expands to `nil` (for a
    syntactic, assertion-shaped form -- there is nothing left to check, so
    there is nothing left to compile: the argument forms are discarded by
    the macro, never analysed, and cost nothing) or an ordinary function
    that throws \"checks are not enabled in this build\" (for anything that
    only makes sense while actually checking something, such as running a
    test suite). A name's KIND (macro or function) is the same in both
    variants; only the body differs.

  Existing `#?(:flint/check ...)` conditionals elsewhere in `lib/` and in
  user code keep working exactly as before -- they still select check-only
  code at those call sites -- this change only means a reference to
  `flint.check` itself no longer NEEDS one.

  ## The predicate

  `expect` takes a predicate and the value to test. A predicate is either an
  ordinary function -- `string?`, `pos?`, anything -- or a value implementing
  `Predicate`, which is what lets a predicate be COMPUTED:

      (expect (http-status 200) response)
      (expect (min-length 3) name)

  Those return values that know both how to test and how to explain
  themselves. An ordinary function cannot: flint drops metadata on a closure,
  so a function has nowhere to carry an implementation. That constraint is the
  reason the protocol is a protocol over VALUES rather than a registry keyed on
  functions.

  ## Why two hooks

  `check` answers, `explain` describes -- and `explain` runs ONLY when `check`
  said no. The happy path allocates nothing, which is what makes it reasonable
  to put these inside the standard library's hot paths. The cost is that a
  failing predicate is evaluated twice; every flint value is immutable, so the
  second look sees exactly what the first did.

  ## No requires

  Deliberately. This namespace is compiled into every build (checks on or
  off), and it is ordered before everything but `clojure.core` -- so anything
  it required would have to be ordered before it too, and the pin that puts
  it early would put it ahead of its own dependency. It joins strings with
  `flint.rt/str-join` rather than `clojure.string` for that reason and no
  other."
  ;; stdcore REFERS NOTHING OF `clojure.core` (`DECISIONS.md#four-units`): it is
  ;; in every image, `clojure.core` is a library only the namespaces referring it
  ;; reach, and what this needs of it lives in `flint.core.impl`.
  (:refer-clojure :only [])
  (:require [flint.core.impl :refer :all]))

;; --------------------------------------------------------------- protocol

;; `check`/`explain` are ordinary FUNCTIONS in both variants (protocol
;; methods dispatch exactly like a function from a caller's point of view --
;; `(check p args)` is one call, inlined to nothing special), so the OFF
;; variant mirrors that kind rather than becoming a macro. They THROW rather
;; than no-op: there is no sensible stand-in answer for "does this predicate
;; pass" when the checking facility itself is the thing switched off, unlike
;; `expect` below, which has an obvious vanish.
#?(:flint/check
   ;; LONGHAND, the way `flint.protocols` writes its own: `defprotocol` and
   ;; `extend-protocol` are `clojure.core`'s, and this namespace refers nothing
   ;; of `clojure.core` (`DECISIONS.md#four-units`) -- it is stdcore, present in
   ;; every image whether or not `clojure.core` is. This is exactly what
   ;; `(defprotocol Predicate (check [p args]) (explain [p args]))` and
   ;; `(extend-protocol Predicate :fn (check [f args] (apply f args)))` expand
   ;; to, written out.
   (do
     (def Predicate__impls (flint.rt/atom {}))
     (def Predicate
       "How a value tests and describes itself.

  `check` must be cheap and allocation-free on success -- it is called on every
  checked call. `explain` is called only after `check` has already said no, so
  it may do as much work as a good message needs.

  Both take the arguments as a VECTOR, because `expect` is variadic: `(expect =
  a b)` and `(expect between? x lo hi)` are ordinary uses, and a protocol whose
  hooks took a single value could not express them."
       (hash-map :flint/protocol 'flint.check/Predicate
                 :impls Predicate__impls
                 :method-keys '[flint.check/check flint.check/explain]))
     (defn check
       "Truthy if `args` pass."
       [p args]
       (let [f (find-protocol-method Predicate__impls 'flint.check/check p)]
         (if f (f p args) (flint.protocols/protocol-miss 'flint.check/Predicate 'flint.check/check p))))
     (defn explain
       "Why `args` failed, as data: `{:expected .. :actual .. :note ..}`. Any
       key may be absent; `expect` renders what it is given."
       [p args]
       (let [f (find-protocol-method Predicate__impls 'flint.check/explain p)]
         (if f (f p args) (flint.protocols/protocol-miss 'flint.check/Predicate 'flint.check/explain p)))))
   :default
   (do
     (defn check
       "Checks are not enabled in this build. Always throws; see `expect`,
       which becomes a free no-op instead of calling this."
       [p args]
       (throw (ex-info "checks are not enabled in this build" {})))
     (defn explain
       "Checks are not enabled in this build. Always throws."
       [p args]
       (throw (ex-info "checks are not enabled in this build" {})))))

#?(:flint/check
   (flint.protocols/extend-method Predicate :fn "check" (fn [f args] (apply f args))))

#?(:flint/check
   (defn- join-lines [xs]
     (flint.rt/str-join (vec (interpose "\n" xs)))))

#?(:flint/check
   (defn- render-value
     "A value as it should appear in a failure. Long ones are cut: an error that
  scrolls off the screen is an error nobody reads."
     [v]
     (let [s (pr-str v)]
       (if (> (count s) 200) (str (subs s 0 200) " ...") s))))

(defn describe
  "The explanation for `p` failing on `args`, as a map.

  A predicate that does not implement `explain` gets one built from what
  `expect` saw in the SOURCE -- which is why the macro carries the predicate
  expression through. `string?` cannot describe itself, but `(expect string? x)`
  can still say `expected  string?`.

  Only makes sense while checking; the OFF variant throws."
  [p args src]
  #?(:flint/check
     (let [f (find-protocol-method (:impls Predicate) 'flint.check/explain p)]
       (or (when f (f p args)) {:expected src}))
     :default
     (throw (ex-info "checks are not enabled in this build" {}))))

#?(:flint/check
   (defn- caret
     "A pointer under the offending expression.

  The source line is not embedded -- the FORM is, and it prints back. So this
  renders what was written rather than quoting it verbatim: whitespace and
  comments are gone, and the column is the one the reader recorded for the
  sub-form, which is exact."
     [col n]
     (str (flint.rt/str-join (vec (repeat col " ")))
          (flint.rt/str-join (vec (repeat (max n 1) "^"))))))

(defn failure-message
  "A check failure, rendered.

  Everything here came from `&form` at macro-expansion: the file, the line, the
  column of the PREDICATE and of the ARGUMENT separately, and both expressions
  as source. No source text is embedded in the image and none is read back at
  run time.

  Only makes sense while checking; the OFF variant throws."
  [site explanation args]
  ;; NO DESTRUCTURING: it expands into `clojure.core/get`, and this namespace
  ;; refers nothing of `clojure.core` (`DECISIONS.md#four-units`).
  #?(:flint/check
     (let [file (:file site) line (:line site) column (:column site) in (:in site)
           pred-src (:pred-src site) arg-src (:arg-src site)
           pred-col (:pred-col site) arg-col (:arg-col site)
           expected (:expected explanation) note (:note explanation)
           head (str "check failed" (when in (str " in " in)))
           where (str "  " file ":" line (when column (str ":" column)))
           form (str "  " pred-src " " arg-src)
           under (when (and pred-col arg-col)
                   (caret (+ 2 (- arg-col pred-col)) (count arg-src)))]
       (join-lines
        (remove nil?
                [head
                 where
                 ""
                 form
                 under
                 ""
                 (str "  expected  " (or expected pred-src))
                 (str "    actual  " (flint.rt/str-join (vec (interpose "  " (map render-value args)))))
                 (when note (str "            " note))])))
     :default
     (throw (ex-info "checks are not enabled in this build" {}))))

;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ expect

;; SYNTACTIC in both variants -- `expect` stays a MACRO when checks are off,
;; mirroring the ON variant's kind, rather than becoming an ordinary function.
;; It is also the one name here that genuinely VANISHES: an assertion form
;; with nothing to assert expands to a bare `nil`, so neither the predicate
;; nor the argument forms are ever analysed -- no call, no evaluation, no
;; image bytes, the same promise `:optimize [perf]` already made when this
;; whole namespace used to disappear instead.
#?(:flint/check
   (defmacro expect
     "Check `args` against `pred`, or throw with a message that says where.

  In a build with checks on, written either bare or inside
  `#?(:flint/check (expect string? name))` -- both work now, and the reader
  conditional form still strips the call entirely under `:optimize [perf]`
  the way it always did. A BARE call, with no conditional, compiles in both
  builds: this macro's OFF variant expands it to `nil`.

      (expect string? name)
      (expect = expected actual)
      (expect (min-length 3) name)

  VARIADIC, because a predicate of one argument is a special case and not the
  general one. The protocol takes a vector of arguments for the same reason.

  The message is built from `&form`: flint's reader records `:file`, `:line`
  and `:column` on every form AND on every sub-form, so the predicate and each
  argument know where they were written. Nothing is embedded but the
  expressions themselves -- there is no copy of the source line in the image,
  and none is read back at run time."
     [pred & args]
     (let [fm (meta &form)
           ;; `:child-pos` is `[line col line col ...]`, one pair per element of
           ;; the form -- so element 0 is `expect` itself, 1 is the predicate and
           ;; 2 is the first argument.
           ;;
           ;; It exists because a NUMBER cannot carry metadata, here or in
           ;; Clojure, so `(expect string? 42)` has nowhere on the 42 to record
           ;; where the 42 is. The collection records it instead, which is the
           ;; difference between pointing at the offending value and not.
           cp (:child-pos fm)
           col-of (fn [i v]
                    (or (:column (meta v))
                        (when (and cp (> (count cp) (inc (* 2 i)))) (nth cp (inc (* 2 i))))))
           site {:file (:file fm)
                 :line (:line fm)
                 :column (:column fm)
                 :in (str (:ns &env))
                 :pred-src (pr-str pred)
                 :arg-src (flint.rt/str-join (vec (interpose " " (map pr-str args))))
                 :pred-col (col-of 1 pred)
                 :arg-col (col-of 2 (first args))}]
       ;; BUILTINS, NOT `clojure.core`, in what lands in the caller: the
       ;; expansion must mean the same in a namespace that refers nothing of
       ;; `clojure.core` (`DECISIONS.md#four-units`), and `clojure.core/first`
       ;; and `ex-info` are these two builtins with a var around them.
       ;; `clojure.core/let` is the BOOTSTRAP `let`, resolved before any var is.
       (list 'clojure.core/let ['p# pred 'a# (vec args)]
             (list 'if (list 'flint.check/check 'p# 'a#)
                   (list 'flint.rt/first 'a#)
                   (list 'throw
                         (list 'flint.rt/ex-info
                               (list 'flint.check/failure-message
                                     site
                                     (list 'flint.check/describe 'p# 'a# (:pred-src site))
                                     'a#)
                               {:flint.check/site site}))))))
   :default
   (defmacro expect
     "Checks are not enabled in this build: this expands to a bare `nil`.
  Neither `pred` nor `args` is ever analysed, let alone evaluated -- an
  assertion with nothing to assert costs nothing, the same promise this whole
  namespace used to keep only by not existing at all."
     [pred & args]
     nil))

;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ tests

(defn test-var?
  "Is this var one of the grouped checks `flint test` runs?

  The compiler indexes EVERY var's metadata, not just this key -- see
  `flint.compiler.core`. This is one client of that index; a doc generator or a
  lint pass would be another asking the same map a different question.

  IDENTICAL in both variants: it is a pure read of a var's own metadata, which
  the compiler indexes unconditionally, so it does not depend on whether
  checks are compiled into THIS build at all."
  [m]
  (boolean (:flint.check/test m)))

(defn run-tests
  "Run `tests` (`[{:var .. :fn ..}]`) and report.

  Takes the registry as an ARGUMENT rather than naming it, because this
  namespace is analysed before any namespace that could have a test in it --
  the registry is generated last, and hands itself here.

  A test passes by returning; it fails by throwing, which is what `expect`
  does. There is no assertion count and no framework: a check that has to be
  registered with something is a check that can be forgotten to register.

  Only makes sense while checking -- there is no registry to run without it,
  since the compiler generates `flint.check.registry` only when `:flint/check`
  is in `features` -- so the OFF variant throws."
  [tests]
  #?(:flint/check
     (loop [ts (seq tests) passed 0 failed 0 out []]
       (if (nil? ts)
         (let [total (+ passed failed)]
           ;; `remove nil?`, because `when` answers nil and `str-join` wants
           ;; strings -- which it says plainly, three frames from the `when`.
           (flint.rt/str-join
            (vec (remove nil?
                         (concat out
                                 [(str "\n" passed "/" total " checks passed")
                                  (when (pos? failed) (str ", " failed " FAILED"))
                                  "\n"])))))
         (let [t (first ts)
               r (try ((:fn t)) nil (catch Throwable e (or (ex-message e) "threw")))]
           (if (nil? r)
             (recur (next ts) (inc passed) failed (conj out (str "  ok   " (:var t) "\n")))
             (recur (next ts) passed (inc failed)
                    (conj out (str "  FAIL " (:var t) "\n" r "\n")))))))
     :default
     (throw (ex-info "checks are not enabled in this build" {}))))
